# {{ index .Caps "visual.evidence" }}/ — what the interface actually looks like

Screenshots captured as proof that an interface change does what its spec said.

## Why this exists

An agent can make an interface change, run the tests, and report success while the
result is unusable — overlapping text, a button off-screen, a modal behind its
backdrop. Nothing in a diff or a test suite catches that, and the person reviewing has
no way to see it without checking out the branch and running the app.

So: if a spec sets `ui: true`, it must link screenshots here, one per acceptance
criterion that describes something visible.

## What this is not

There are no baseline images and no pixel diffing. That was a deliberate choice:

- A pixel-diff suite fails on every intentional change, so it needs a blessing
  workflow, and blessing becomes reflexive within about two weeks. At that point it
  catches nothing and costs everybody time.
- Binary baselines make the repository heavy and the diffs unreadable.
- The interesting failures — this is confusing, this is the wrong emphasis, this
  breaks at 320px — are not pixel differences at all.

A person looks at the screenshots. That is the whole mechanism, and it works because
looking at four images is fast while checking out a branch is not.

## Naming

```
{{ index .Caps "visual.evidence" }}/<spec-id>/<state>.png
```

For example `evidence/spec-checkout-redesign/empty-cart.png`. The state name should say
what a reader is looking at, not what component produced it.

## What to capture

The states a user will actually be in, including the ones that are easy to skip:

- Empty, loading, error, and permission-denied states.
- The narrowest supported viewport, if the project supports more than one.
- Anything the spec's acceptance criteria describe in visual terms.

The happy path alone is not evidence. It is the state that was easiest to reach.
